# SYNOPSIS

```usage
yes [string]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`yes` prints <string>, or `y` if there is none, on a line of its own, again and again, until it is killed.
Words after <string> are ignored.
In a pipe it ends when the reader has gone, killed by the `SIGPIPE` its next write raises.

`yes` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/yes`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/yes`.
There is no native `yes`.

# SEE ALSO

xargs(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/yes.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A flag prints the usage message and exits 1.
`yes` never exits by itself.

# BUGS

Write errors are ignored: with `SIGPIPE` ignored or caught, `yes` goes on forever after its reader has gone.

`yes` is untested on VectraOS.
